  Module: uidesign/ssd1322-256x64/u8g2gl/screens/screen_diagnostics.xml
  Description: The diagnostics board: hostname (bold, centred), WiFi line,
               uptime, and heap plus version, on the 256x64 SSD1322.

  Geometry is chosen to reproduce the legacy DiagnosticsBoard pixel for pixel,
  defects included: these widths and heights reproduce the legacy clip bug
  for bug on purpose, because the migration requires pixel identity. Fixing the
  clip later means growing each label by one pixel in each direction. Every
  number below is measured, see the render harness (test/render/) and section 1
  of .agents/skills/principal-architect-workspace/pr-u8g2gl-05/evidence/EVIDENCE.md.
-->
<screen name="diagnostics">
    <view>
        <!-- Why every height is one less than the legacy label's, and every
             width one less on the data rows (section 1 of the EVIDENCE.md cited above).

             Legacy drawText() clips to setClipWindow(x, y, x + w - 1, y + h - 1)
             with h = getMaxCharHeight(). U8G2 treats that far corner as
             EXCLUSIVE (u8g2.h:352/354, U8G2 2.36.18), so the last column and
             the last row were never drawn. The U8G2GL runtime converts a rect {x, y, w, h}
             to setClipWindow(x, y, x + w, y + h) (u8g2gl_apply_clip()), so its
             last row and column ARE drawn. To clip where legacy really clipped,
             the rect must be one smaller than legacy's argument. Measured under
             the legacy display state (setFontRefHeightAll):
               helvB12_tr getMaxCharHeight() = 17 -> clipped to 16 rows;
               helvR08_tr getMaxCharHeight() = 11 -> clipped to 10 rows.
             That is what cuts the descenders ("My" renders as "Mv"). Of the 11
             pixels the clip removes from the `connected` frame, 5 are the
             exclusive corner's doing; the other 6 lie below the label's own
             height and would go even with an inclusive corner
             (test/render/after/diagnostics_board/clip_probe.json).

             Vertical placement: u8g2gl_label draws at y + (height - (ascent -
             descent)) / 2. ascent - descent is 17 and 11, so (16 - 17) / 2 and
             (10 - 11) / 2 both truncate to 0 and the text sits at y, exactly
             where legacy drew it. -->

        <!-- Row 1: hostname. Legacy: x=0 y=0 w=256, centred.
             width stays 256, not 255, though the clip arithmetic above would
             give 255. Centring is x + (width - textWidth) / 2 and legacy
             centred in 256, so 255 moves the text 1px left whenever
             256 - textWidth is even. Measured: "esp32-foundation" (137px) agrees
             either way, but the framework's own default hostname
             "esp32-device" (104px) would start at column 75 instead of 76.
             The cost of 256 is that the clip reaches column 255 where
             legacy's stopped at 254; that only shows for a hostname wider
             than 255px, which 32 characters of bold 12pt can be. -->
        <u8g2gl_label name="hostname" x="0" y="0" width="256" height="16"
                      font="u8g2_font_helvB12_tr" align="center" bind_text="diag_hostname" />

        <!-- Row 2: WiFi line. Legacy: x=4 y=16 w=248 -> clip columns 4..250.
             width 247 gives the same columns. long_mode stays at its default,
             clip: legacy never set truncation, so a long SSID is cut mid-glyph
             at the clip with no ellipsis. -->
        <u8g2gl_label name="wifi" x="4" y="16" width="247" height="10"
                      font="u8g2_font_helvR08_tr" bind_text="diag_wifi" />

        <!-- Row 3: uptime. Legacy: x=4 y=30. -->
        <u8g2gl_label name="uptime" x="4" y="30" width="247" height="10"
                      font="u8g2_font_helvR08_tr" bind_text="diag_uptime" />

        <!-- Row 4: free heap and firmware version. Legacy: x=4 y=44. -->
        <u8g2gl_label name="heap" x="4" y="44" width="247" height="10"
                      font="u8g2_font_helvR08_tr" bind_text="diag_heap" />
    </view>
</screen>
